  "knowledgePattern": "Count increasing arithmetic triples by their positive common difference",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Name the first term and gap",
      "body": "<p>Write an increasing arithmetic progression as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>. Both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> are positive integers, and the last number cannot exceed 10.</p>",
      "recap": "An arithmetic progression has the same difference between consecutive terms.",
      "pitfall": "The three numbers must be distinct, so the gap cannot be zero.",
      "pause": "Find the possible values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> and count the allowed starting values for each.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l08",
          "reminder": "An arithmetic progression has the same difference between consecutive terms."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Count all possible selections",
      "body": "<p>The order in which the numbers were selected does not matter. Count all three-number selections with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "A combination counts a choice of positions or objects without ordering them.",
      "pitfall": "Use unordered selections in both the successful count and the total count.",
      "pause": "Divide your count of arithmetic progressions by the total number of selections.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l11",
          "reminder": "A combination counts a choice of positions or objects without ordering them."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> Each unordered set of three distinct numbers is equally likely, so the sample space has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>120</mn></mrow></math> choices.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> An increasing arithmetic triple has the unique form <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, with positive integers a,d and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>. Therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, allowing respectively 8,6,4,2 starting values.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> There are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></math> favourable triples, with no duplication because each has one least member and one positive common difference. The probability is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>120</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>. Answer C.</p>"
}
